I've long passed being challenged by the usual list
there. Falling down a trap door probably means I've
abandoned the protection racket anyway, at least
until I find my pet again, though I try to keep it
until I find or reach the up stairs. Unless I lose
my pet or it loses me, there simply are no big scary
monsters until below Mines Town that a pet cannot
handle, so long as the PC is willing to retreat up
the stairs with the pet once in a while for some
"heal together" time. A healer has a direct
measurement of the health of its pet available for
free every single turn they are adjacent to one
another, and sadly, I'm usually more aware of my
pet's HP than of my own.

Sometimes the run down to Sokoban at XL:1 has to be
abandoned, when Sokoban's entrance is deep and the
monster level starts to become unmanageable. Then
it's time to revise the plan, back up, and go do the
upper levels of Mines for some excellent armor. With
patience, the RNG will _always_ provide. Meanwhile,
the PC is exploring, snatching food from the pet
(easier in more open areas), and avoiding remote
attacks. At every level of mines, the PC does a
stairs hover until the kit of monsters generated at
level entry time is exhausted, then goes out to
encounter the (still wussy) newly generated monsters
one by one. A certain amount of cat herding is
needed to make this work, and that part is a bit
boring, but having kitty get to the monster before I
do is a real strategic win.

Too, by now I've often got healing to cast on the
pet, either from having started with PW:(5/5), or
from having upgraded the two lesser starting values
to that amount with newt corpses. The pet being kept
in good health speeds the rate at which further
exploration can occur, because the pet can be let
free to conquer monsters. Keeping the pet in front as
much as possible decreases the traps found by the PC
before being found by the pet quite a bit, since the
pet moves faster and covers more territory. If I
were an obsessively careful player, I'd map where my
pet has stepped, and try to step only on the same
spots, but I don't, and sometimes I pay for that
choice. Ditto searching at every step.

If I've made sure to search DLVL:1 to DLVL:4 before
entering Mines, and given my pet pretty much all the
kills, my pet is usually a 9 hit die monster by the
second level of Mines, and as ready to deal with the
Mines Town captain as it will ever be. If I have two
pets at that HP:(72/72) level, and a leash, I can be
sure they take on the watch captain together or not
at all, with me casting healing as needed, and
rarely lose a pet to that buff monster any more.
 
